    "text": "It can be changed by lifting the blockade and ending the occupation. The blockade ended his possibilities to trade and sustain his family. That same year, some members of the community blockaded a road to a nearby diamond mine. Obstacles this way, blockades that way, and bridges burnt behind. The government and eyewitnesses disagree about how the blockade ended. Ranch trucks occasionally drove by, but the few reporters outside the blockade were told no one was being let in. The group said it found hundreds of people in famine-like conditions it blamed on government blockades preventing food and supplies from entering. The authorities also realized that hard-line measures and broadcast blockades would only worsen their position and that they must take their critics into account. The official information blockade only contributed to the protest mood. Other demonstrations are planned in other parts of the city, but the blockades that crippled traffic yesterday have now receded. In the next step, the researchers investigated the blockade mechanism on more complex models, including a large protein fragment, similar to previous experiments. In its wake, the massive blockades like those during 1991 became a thing of the past. We will continue our fight against the financial blockade, and we will continue to publish. Some legal scholars, however, view the aged and restrictive laws governing blockades as being out of step with modern conflict. Leaving aside the larger question of the blockade's legitimacy, the entire episode could have been handled without drama. These examples are from corpora and from sources on the web. Any opinions in the examples do not represent the opinion of the Cambridge Dictionary editors or of Cambridge University Press or its licensors.",
